a final moment of the uk's 20-year  military mission in afghanistan only  made public now  two weeks after the event the secrecy  apparently required because of security  concerns  instead of marching out victorious the  uk and other nato forces  have quietly departed as the taliban  gains ground  and fears of a civil war grow boris  johnson insisted the intervention  had made the uk safer from a terrorist  threat  once rooted in afghanistan no one should  doubt the gains  of the last 20 years but nor can we  shrink  from the hard reality of the situation  today  the international military presence in  afghanistan was never  intended to be permanent the timeline  for leaving was set by this man who said  his troops will be out by the end of  august  the united states did what we want to do  in afghanistan  to get the terrorists to attack us on 9  11  and deliver justice to osama bin laden  we're ending  america's longest war  but we'll always always honor the  bravery of the american patriots who  served it  he said taliban forces are at their  strongest since the invasion  here sky news was taken to what had been  an afghan army base in central wadack  province  now occupied by the taliban though  president biden said he didn't think a  complete taliban takeover was inevitable  more than 150 000 british forces served  in the u.s led war in afghanistan since  2001.  457 died while more than 2  400 american personnel perished as well  as almost  70 000 afghan security force members  this airbase north of kabul was once the  hub of the u.s military presence  now a husk handed back to the afghans  the intervention did improve the  standard of living for many  and deprived al qaeda of a haven to plot  acts of terror  that can that legacy endure with the  foreign firepower gone  clearly we've failed in our attempts to  stabilize afghanistan  and set it up for the future and so for  all those  my friends and people from here and all  over the world that have attempted  um and sacrificed an awful lot lives in  many cases  uh to deliver that outcome it's going to  be very hard for them to feel that it's  worth it  but the story of afghanistan isn't over  yet for this chapter though  journalists weren't allowed to attend  the uk's farewell ceremonies  or speak to troops on the ground afghans  now standing alone  even as britain says it's not turning  away  haynes sky news
 

